al pathological state nouns like deficiency, spasticity, abnormal development that lack a disease-head noun but appear as complements of characterized-by/results-in/secondary-to predicates should inherit DiseaseClass status, while the model instead defaults to treating them as neutral clinical background and tags them O. I'm verifying this hypothesis holds across each example — deficiency of norrin fits the pattern just as abnormal development did.\n\nSo the rule crystallizes: the model only succeeds when a term is a lexicalized disorder name like \"mental retardation,\" but fails when it's a sign or mechanism nominal like spasticity or deficiency. I'm drafting the final pattern insight — naming it something like \"Disease-Manifestation Nominals in Characterization/Causal Clauses,\" specifying the confusion trigger as bare sign/deficiency/abnormality nominals sitting as complements of disease-defining predicates that lack an explicit disease head noun, contrasting this against correctly-tagged nosological terms, and proposing a linguistic rule that such spans (including their full modifier/PP span) should be labeled DiseaseClass when they describe a patient's pathological state tied to a SpecificDisease subject, while excluding neutral methodological or study-variable nouns.\n\n", "type": "thinking"}, {"text": "**Step 1: Deep Dive Analysis**\n\n- **Contrast:** All three discrepancies are *disease-manifestation nominals*: \"abnormal retinal vascular development,\" \"deficiency of norrin,\" and \"spasticity.\" Each is the complement of a disease-defining predicate (\"is characterized by,\" \"results in,\" \"secondary to\" a named disease subject). The true-positive DiseaseClass span \"mental retardation\" sits in the same list, in the same sentence, under the same predicate, and the model got it right.\n- **Isolate trigger:** Syntax does not separate the cases, since \"spasticity\" and \"mental retardation\" share a frame. The difference is lexical-semantic. \"Mental 